/// Chapter 14 of the guide, written from md-docs/learn/09-control-flow.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page ControlFlowGuide(path: "/docs/guide/control-flow", title: "Control flow", description: "if, if let, for … by, VirtualList, show and match, in render blocks and in actions, and if and match as values.", layout: DocsShell("Control flow", chapter: t("nav.group.concepts"), blurb: "Five statements decide what a page shows: if, if let, for, show and match. Each adds and removes elements as its condition changes.", source: "md-docs/learn/09-control-flow.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "if-else-if-else", label: "if / else if / else"), Anchor(id: "if-let", label: "if let"), Anchor(id: "for", label: "for"), Anchor(id: "show", label: "show"), Anchor(id: "match", label: "match"), Anchor(id: "expressions-that-choose", label: "Expressions that choose"), Anchor(id: "in-actions-and-handlers", label: "In actions and handlers"), Anchor(id: "summary", label: "Summary")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/events", prevLabel: t("ch.13"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/components", nextLabel: t("ch.15"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("Five statements decide what a page shows: `if`, `if let`, `for`, `show` and `match`. Each one follows the state it reads, adding and removing elements as its condition changes, so you never update the page by hand. Each animates when you ask it to ([Motion](/docs/guide/motion)).\n\nIn this chapter:\n\n- `if`, `else if` and `else`, and `if let` for a value that may be null\n- `for` over a list, with a key — and `VirtualList` for thousands of rows\n- `show`, which hides elements without removing them\n- `match`, which shows one arm by an enum's case\n- `if` and `match` as values, and the same statements in actions\n\nThe statements in this chapter appear in a *render block* — the body of a page, component or element, which says what to draw. The last section shows them in an *imperative block* — a handler or an action, which runs code.")

        Heading("if / else if / else", id: "if-else-if-else").h2

        Markdown("`if` shows a branch while its condition holds. This page shows one of three messages, by how many times the button was pressed:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Status(path: "/", title: "Status", description: "A message chosen by a count.") {
    state count = 0
    Heading("Status").h1
    if count == 0 {
        Text("Nothing yet.").muted
    } else if count < 10 {
        Text("A few: {count}")
    } else {
        Text("Many: {count}").bold
    }
    Button("+1") { on click { count = count + 1 } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("How it behaves:\n\n- A branch's elements are created when its condition turns true, and removed when it turns false.\n- State declared inside a component in the branch starts over each time the branch is created.\n- A condition must be a `Bool`, or a value that may be null, which counts as `false` when it is null.\n- A list or a record as a condition is an error (`T07`), since it would always be true.")

        Heading("if let", id: "if-let").h2

        Markdown("`if let name = value \{ \}` binds the value inside the branch when it is not null. It is the way to read a value of type `T?` (one that may be null) without the type checker objecting:")

        CodeBlock(#"type User { name: String, email: String? }

page Profile(path: "/", title: "Profile", description: "A user who may not be signed in.") {
    state user: User? = null
    Heading("Profile").h1
    if let u = user {
        Text("Signed in as {u.name}")
        if let mail = u.email { Text(mail).muted } else { Text("No email on file").muted }
    } else {
        Button("Sign in") { on click { user = User(name: "Sam", email: null) } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The rules:\n\n- `if let x \{ \}`, with no `=`, does the same for a name already in scope.\n- The narrowing holds inside the branch only. `if x != null \{ \}` and `if x && … \{ \}` narrow `x` inside their branch the same way; the right-hand side of the `&&` is not narrowed.\n- The binding follows the value. When `user` becomes another user, the branch shows the new one in place. It is drawn again only when the value goes to `null` and back.")

        Heading("for", id: "for").h2

        Markdown("`for` draws its block once for each item of a list. This page loops three ways — by key, with the index, and over a range:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Todo { id: String, title: String, done: Bool = false }

page Checklist(path: "/", title: "Checklist", description: "Three kinds of loop.") {
    state todos: [Todo] = [Todo(id: "a", title: "Write"), Todo(id: "b", title: "Ship", done: true)]
    Heading("Checklist").h1

    for todo in todos by todo.id {                      // keyed: each item keeps its nodes
        Row(gap: .sm) {
            Checkbox(checked: todo.done, label: todo.title) { on change { todo.done = !todo.done } }
        }
    }
    for todo, i in todos {                              // with the index
        Text("{i + 1}. {todo.title}")
    }
    for n in 1..=3 { Badge("{n}") }                     // over a range
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`by expr` names the *key*: the value that tells one item from another. With a key:\n\n- An item that stays in the list keeps its nodes. Focus, scroll position and animation state survive an insert, a removal or a move, and moves slide.\n- An item keeps its nodes under a new value too — a refreshed response's rows are all new objects. Its body reads the item and the index live, so a changed row changes in place, and its components' state with it.\n- Keys must be unique. The runtime warns once when two items share one.\n- A key may read the index as well as the item (`for t, i in rows by \"\{t.id\}-\{i\}\"`).\n\nWithout a key, the list is rebuilt whenever it changes. Looping over something that is not a list is an error (`T08`).")

        Callout("Do not key by the index alone. A key of the index moves with the position, not the item: after an insert every item has another's key, and nothing is kept. Key by what identifies the item.", kind: .warning)

        Heading("Long lists", id: "long-lists").h3

        Markdown("A list of thousands of rows — a project's files, a search's results, a long diff — belongs inside a **`VirtualList`**. The box scrolls, and only the rows in view and a few either side are drawn; two spacers stand for the rest, so the scrollbar is the whole list's. Its body is one keyed `for`.\n\nThis page windows ten thousand file paths of a fixed height, and a list of search hits whose rows are measured:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Hit { id: String, heading: Bool, text: String }

page Search(path: "/", title: "Search", description: "Results, a window at a time.") {
    state rows = (0..10000).map((i) => "src/file{i}.wf")
    state hits: [Hit] = []
    state current = ""
    Heading("Search").h1
    VirtualList(rowHeight: 32, label: "Files") {
        style { height: 400px }
        for path in rows by path { Text(path) { style { height: 32px; margin: 0 } } }
    }
    VirtualList(estimate: 24, reveal: current, label: "Results") {
        style { height: 400px }
        for h in hits by h.id {
            if h.heading { Heading(h.text).h2 } else { Text(h.text) }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Its props:\n\n- **`rowHeight:`** says every row is that tall. It is the fast path.\n- Without `rowHeight:`, each row is measured once drawn, and **`estimate:`** stands for the rows not yet drawn. When a row above what is in view turns out taller or shorter, the scroll moves by as much, so what the reader is looking at stays put.\n- **`reveal:`** reads a row's key. When it changes, that row is scrolled into view — the selected file as the arrow keys move, the current match.\n- **`label:`** is the box's accessible name.\n\nHow it draws:\n\n- A row keeps its nodes while it stays in the window, and is updated in place when its item changes, as a keyed `for`'s rows are. A row scrolled out of the window is let go.\n- Give rows no vertical margins: a row's height is its box's.\n- A tree is a list of the rows that are showing. Flatten it in a `derived` (each open folder followed by its children) and window that.\n- A list drawn before its box is laid out — inside an `if` that has turned true, in a panel not shown yet — draws only the few rows it keeps past the window until the box has a height. The first height it has is laid out before that frame is painted, so the first frame shows the rows in view, not a guess at them trimmed a frame later. A row `reveal:` asks for in the meantime is scrolled to then, so that frame shows it. A list hidden and shown again is laid out the same way.\n- The box takes what any element takes ([Elements](/docs/guide/elements)): a `role: \"listbox\"`, a `tabindex:`, an `aria-activedescendant:` that follows the current row, `data-*`, and its `on` handlers — an `on keydown` that moves through a list of references, for example.\n- The pre-rendered page paints the first 60 rows. The runtime draws the window when it takes over.\n- With anything else in its body, or a `for` with no `by`, it draws every row (`V12`).")

        Callout("The box needs a height that does not come from its rows — `style \{ height: 400px \}`, a `max-height:`, or a grid cell or flex row that sets one. A box as tall as what it holds grows until every row is drawn.", kind: .important)

        Heading("show", id: "show").h2

        Markdown("`show` keeps its elements in the page and toggles whether they are visible; `if` creates and destroys them. Use `show` for something expensive to rebuild, or whose state must survive being hidden:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Panel(path: "/", title: "Panel", description: "A panel hidden, not removed.") {
    state open = true
    Heading("Panel").h1
    Button("Toggle") { on click { open = !open } }
    show open {
        Card { Text("Still here when hidden; my state survives.") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("match", id: "match").h2

        Markdown("`match` shows one arm at a time. Over an **enum** it chooses by case, and a case with a *payload* — values carried by the case — hands them to its arm. This job moves through its cases as the buttons are pressed:")

        CodeBlock(#"enum Status { idle, running(job: String), failed(reason: String), done(count: Number) }

page Job(path: "/", title: "Job", description: "One arm per case of an enum.") {
    state s: Status = .idle
    Heading("Job").h1
    match s {
        .idle { Button("Start") { on click { s = .running("build-42") } } }
        .running(job) { Row { Spinner.sm  Text("Running {job}")  Button("Fail") { on click { s = .failed("timed out") } } } }
        .failed(reason) { Alert("Failed: {reason}").danger }
        .done(n) { Text("Done, {n} steps") }
    }
    Text(match s { .idle { "waiting" } .failed(r) { r } else { "busy" } })   // as a value
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Over a **resource** it chooses by the request's state — loading, error or ready ([Fetching data](/docs/guide/data)).\n\nThe rules:\n\n- An arm binds one name per part of the payload: `.failed(reason)`.\n- `else` covers every case without an arm of its own.\n- Without an `else`, a `match` must give every case of the enum an arm, or it is an error (`T15`). That holds for a `match` statement and a `match` expression alike; an expression that covers every case needs no `else`.\n- An arm is drawn again when the arm changes, not when its value does. A new payload of the same case — `.failed(\"refused\")` after `.failed(\"timed out\")` — reaches what the arm drew in place.")

        Heading("Expressions that choose", id: "expressions-that-choose").h2

        Markdown("`if` and `match` are also values, and `??` picks a fallback for a value that may be null:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Choose(path: "/", title: "Choose", description: "Values picked by a condition.") {
    state n = 5
    state nick: String? = null
    derived size = if n > 10 { "big" } else { "small" }
    derived label = nick ?? "anonymous"
    Heading("Choose").h1
    Text("{size} {label}")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("An `if` value with no `else` is `null` when its condition fails. [Expressions](/docs/guide/expressions) has the rest.")

        Heading("In actions and handlers", id: "in-actions-and-handlers").h2

        Markdown("The same statements work in an imperative block, on values rather than elements, plus `try`/`catch`. This action fetches rows and keeps the first hundred:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Sync(path: "/", title: "Sync", description: "Control flow inside an action.") {
    state rows: [Map] = []
    state error = ""
    action pull() {
        try {
            let r = await fetch("/api/rows")
            let { items } = r
            rows = []
            for item, i in items {
                if i >= 100 { return }
                rows.push(item)
            }
        } catch e {
            error = e.message
        }
    }
    Heading("Sync").h1
    Button("Pull") { on click { pull() } }
    if error != "" { Alert(error).danger }
    Text("{rows.length} rows")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Summary", id: "summary").h2

        Table(caption: "Summary") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("You write").header
                    Table.Cell("It shows").header
                    Table.Cell("Section").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`if c \{ … \} else if d \{ … \} else \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("the first branch whose condition holds")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[`if`](#if-else-if-else)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`if let u = user \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("a branch with the value, when it is not null")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[`if let`](#if-let)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`for todo in todos by todo.id \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("the block once per item, keyed")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[`for`](#for)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`for t, i in todos \{ … \}`, `for n in 1..=3 \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("with the index; over a range")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[`for`](#for)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`VirtualList(rowHeight: 32) \{ for … by … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("only the rows in view")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Long lists](#long-lists)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`show open \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("its block, hidden rather than removed")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[`show`](#show)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`match s \{ .idle \{ … \} else \{ … \} \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("one arm by case, or by a resource's state")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[`match`](#match)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`if c \{ a \} else \{ b \}`, `match s \{ … \}`, `a ?? b`") }
                    Table.Cell("a value")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Expressions that choose](#expressions-that-choose)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`try \{ … \} catch e \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("in an action: a guard on what may fail")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[In actions and handlers](#in-actions-and-handlers)") }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
